See INSTALL file for instructions on how to install SpeexDSP.

SpeexDSP is a patent-free, Open Source/Free Software DSP library.

Precise rational clock mode (floating-point builds)
====================================================

The resampler normally keeps its fractional source position on the current
ratio's denominator (samp_frac_num/den_rate).  When the ratio changes, that
position has to be rescaled onto the new grid and is truncated in the
process; several A -> B -> A rate changes in a row therefore move the read
point even when no output frame was produced in between.  There is no way to
undo a rate change that has not produced any sound yet.

The optional precise clock mode removes that coupling.  The nominal source
cursor U of the next output frame is stored as a denominator-independent
64.64 fixed-point number (an integer part plus a fraction counted in units of
2^-64 input frames), one cursor per channel.  Setting a new ratio then only
changes the advance applied to the next frame that is actually produced; the
position already accumulated is never rescaled or truncated.  An A -> B -> A
round trip with no generated frame in between is exactly equivalent to never
changing the ratio, including the subsequent audio.

The clock is not a separate query-only ledger: the same 64.64 position drives
the FIR integer read point and the fractional cubic interpolation.  Precise
mode always uses the oversampled sinc table with cubic interpolation, so the
table size does not grow with the ratio denominator.  Its output is not
required to be bit identical to the legacy direct-table path; the filter
window, cutoff and integer interface quantization conventions are unchanged.

Rules:
  * Only FLOATING_POINT builds support it; fixed-point builds reject
    speex_resampler_enable_precise_clock() with RESAMPLER_ERR_BAD_STATE.
  * Enable only on a freshly created state, or after
    speex_resampler_reset_mem(), before any input has been processed.  Once
    enabled it cannot be disabled.
  * speex_resampler_set_rate_frac() then accepts only reduced fractions whose
    numerator and denominator are each <= 1000000 and whose value is in
    [1/2, 2].  Anything else is rejected with RESAMPLER_ERR_INVALID_ARG and
    the live ratio, history and cursor are left untouched.
  * Quality may only be re-selected after speex_resampler_reset_mem();
    changing it mid-stream returns RESAMPLER_ERR_BAD_STATE.
  * reset_mem() clears the filter history and every channel cursor (U = 0),
    but keeps precise mode enabled and keeps the current ratio.

Cursor versus latency
---------------------
U is the nominal read position of the next output frame.  It is zero after
creation and after reset_mem(), and advances by ratio_num/ratio_den for every
output frame actually generated.  Querying the cursor, changing the ratio, a
zero output capacity and calls that produce no output do not advance it.

U deliberately excludes the startup filter delay.
speex_resampler_skip_zeros() only suppresses the leading zero outputs caused
by that delay; it never moves U.  The filter delay is reported separately by
speex_resampler_get_input_latency() and speex_resampler_get_output_latency().
Do not add the latency to U when locating the source sample of an output
frame: U already is that nominal position, while the latency describes how
many frames the filter buffers internally.

For any run of up to 2^20 generated frames the cursor differs from exact
rational accumulation by at most 2^-40 input frames.  Each channel advances
independently through the per-channel entry points; the interleaved entry
points advance every channel by the same output frame count.

C example
---------

    #include <speex/speex_resampler.h>
    #include <stdint.h>
    #include <stdio.h>

    int main(void)
    {
       int err = 0;
       SpeexResamplerState *st = speex_resampler_init_frac(
             1, 2, 3, 32000, 48000,
             SPEEX_RESAMPLER_QUALITY_DESKTOP, &err);
       if (!st) return 1;

       if (speex_resampler_enable_precise_clock(st) != RESAMPLER_ERR_SUCCESS)
       {
          speex_resampler_destroy(st);
          return 1;
       }

       /* Changing the ratio before any frame is produced is reversible. */
       speex_resampler_set_rate_frac(st, 5, 7, 32000, 48000);
       speex_resampler_set_rate_frac(st, 2, 3, 32000, 48000);

       /* ... feed frames with speex_resampler_process_float() ... */

       spx_uint64_t whole, fraction;
       if (speex_resampler_get_precise_clock(st, 0, &whole, &fraction)
             == RESAMPLER_ERR_SUCCESS)
       {
          double U = (double)whole + (double)fraction / 18446744073709551616.0;
          printf("nominal source cursor of next frame: %.9f input frames\n", U);
       }

       speex_resampler_destroy(st);
       return 0;
    }

